Conditional rendering displays different UI depending on application state:

• Ternary Operator: {isAuth ? <Dashboard /> : <Login />}
• Logical AND (&&): {hasUnread && <Badge count={unreadCount} />}
• Early Return: Return null or a skeleton loader before main JSX.
• Switch / Lookup Objects: For multi-step wizards and status screens.

The cleanup function is the function returned from the useEffect callback:

useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(...);
    return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, []);

It runs right before the component unmounts AND before re-running the effect on subsequent renders when dependencies change, preventing memory leaks and orphaned subscriptions.

useMemo caches the result of an expensive calculation between renders:

const total = useMemo(() => calculateTotal(items), [items]);

It only re-executes the calculation when one of the specified dependencies changes. Use it for heavy filtering, sorting, or complex mathematical transformations.

useCallback caches a function definition between renders:

const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);

In JavaScript, functions declared inside a component are recreated with new memory references on every render. useCallback preserves referential equality so child components wrapped in React.memo don't re-render needlessly.

useRef returns a mutable object with a .current property that persists across renders:

const inputRef = useRef(null);
<input ref={inputRef} />
inputRef.current.focus();

Assigning it to a JSX ref attribute gives direct access to the underlying DOM node without causing component re-renders.

A Custom Hook is a JavaScript function whose name starts with 'use' and can call other React Hooks:

function useWindowWidth() {
    const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);
    useEffect(() => {
        const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
        window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
        return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    }, []);
    return width;
}

Custom hooks encapsulate and share stateful logic between components cleanly.

Give each input a 'name' attribute matching the state object property key:

const handleChange = (e) => {
    const { name, value } = e.target;
    setFormData(prev => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));
};

Using dynamic property keys ([name]: value) avoids creating separate handlers for every field.

Use the @hookform/resolvers/zod package:

const schema = z.object({
    email: z.string().email(),
    password: z.string().min(8)
});

const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm({
    resolver: zodResolver(schema)
});

This automatically validates inputs against the Zod schema and generates TypeScript types.

In React Hook Form, use custom async validation rules:

<input {...register('username', {
    validate: async (val) => {
        const isAvailable = await checkUsername(val);
        return isAvailable || 'Username is already taken';
    }
})} />

Debounce the API call to avoid spamming the backend server on every keystroke.

Call the reset() function provided by useForm:

const onSubmit = async (data) => {
    await api.post('/users', data);
    reset(); // Clears all inputs and reset dirty/touched states
};

You can also pass default values: reset({ name: '', email: '' }).

Using template literals or the clsx / classnames library:

// Template literal:
className={`btn ${isActive ? 'btn-active' : ''}`}

// With clsx library:
className={clsx('btn', { 'btn-active': isActive, 'btn-disabled': isDisabled })}

CSS-in-JS writes CSS styles directly inside JavaScript using tagged template literals:

const Button = styled.button`
    background: ${props => props.primary ? 'blue' : 'gray'};
    padding: 10px 20px;
`;

It generates unique classes, handles vendor prefixing, and allows dynamic styling based on component props.

useSearchParams works similarly to useState:

const [searchParams, setSearchParams] = useSearchParams();
const query = searchParams.get('q');

// Update query param:
setSearchParams({ q: 'react', page: '2' });

const navigate = useNavigate();

// Navigate to path:
navigate('/dashboard');

// Navigate back:
navigate(-1);

// Replace history entry:
navigate('/login', { replace: true });

Create an AuthGuard wrapper component:

const ProtectedRoute = () => {
    const { isAuthenticated } = useAuth();
    return isAuthenticated ? <Outlet /> : <Navigate to='/login' replace />;
};

Wrap secure routes inside: <Route element={<ProtectedRoute />}>.

Pass the current location in state when redirecting:
<Navigate to='/login' state={{ from: location }} replace />

After login success, read the previous path:
const from = location.state?.from?.pathname || '/dashboard';
navigate(from, { replace: true });

1. Create: const ThemeContext = createContext('light');
2. Provide: <ThemeContext.Provider value={theme}>{children}</ThemeContext.Provider>
3. Consume: const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);

const useCart = create((set) => ({
    items: [],
    addItem: (item) => set((state) => ({ items: [...state.items, item] })),
    clear: () => set({ items: [] })
}));

// In component:
const items = useCart((state) => state.items);

Use Zustand's built-in persist middleware:

const useStore = create(
    persist(
        (set) => ({ count: 0 }),
        { name: 'app-storage' }
    )
);

useEffect(() => {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    axios.get('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal })
        .then(res => setData(res.data))
        .catch(err => { if (!axios.isCancel(err)) handleError(err); });
    return () => controller.abort(); // Cancels on unmount
}, []);

An HOC is a pure function that takes a component as an argument and returns an enhanced component:

const withAuth = (WrappedComponent) => {
    return (props) => {
        const { user } = useAuth();
        return user ? <WrappedComponent {...props} /> : <Redirect />;
    };
};

Used for cross-cutting concerns like logging, permissions, and theme injection.

Concurrent React allows rendering tasks to be interrupted, paused, and resumed.

useTransition marks a state update as a non-urgent transition:
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
startTransition(() => setFilter(searchQuery));

Keeps UI inputs responsive even while rendering heavy list updates.

Use renderHook from @testing-library/react:

const { result } = renderHook(() => useCounter());
act(() => {
    result.current.increment();
});
expect(result.current.count).toBe(1);

act() ensures state updates and effects are flushed before assertions run.

Establish connection inside useEffect and clean up on unmount:

useEffect(() => {
    const socket = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com');
    socket.onmessage = (e) => setMessages(prev => [...prev, e.data]);
    return () => socket.close(); // Clean up
}, []);

const formData = new FormData();
formData.append('file', selectedFile);
await axios.post('/api/upload', formData, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data' },
    onUploadProgress: (p) => setProgress(Math.round((p.loaded * 100) / p.total))
});
